Executive Summary--

Short-Term Ferry Ticket Demand Forecasting & Predictive Decision Support System

Toronto Island Park ferry operations face recurring challenges in managing sudden spikes in passenger demand.
Current practices rely heavily on reactive responses, which increase the risk of congestion, delays, and safety concerns.
This project introduces a predictive intelligence framework that transforms historical ticket data into actionable short-term forecasts.

Purpose--
The system provides ferry operators with 15-minute to 2-hour demand forecasts, enabling proactive scheduling, staff allocation, and crowd management. By integrating machine learning and time-series models, the solution shifts operations from reactive to predictive planning.

Key Objectives--

- Primary

Forecast short-term ticket sales and redemptions

Predict demand for upcoming 15-minute to 2-hour windows

Compare statistical and machine-learning forecasting approaches

- Secondary

Quantify prediction uncertainty with confidence intervals

Support proactive operational planning

Demonstrate real-world ML deployment via a Streamlit dashboard

Benefits for Stakeholders--

Operational Efficiency: Align ferry schedules with real-time demand forecasts.

Safety Preparedness: Anticipate crowd surges and deploy staff proactively.

Public Service Optimization: Reduce wait times, improve passenger experience, and enhance trust in public transport services.

Decision Support: Provide government stakeholders with transparent KPIs (accuracy, peak miss rate, forecast lead time) to evaluate system reliability.

Deliverables--

Streamlit Dashboard: Interactive forecasting tool with model comparison and confidence bands.

Research Paper: Analytical insights and recommendations for operational planning.

Executive Summary: Strategic overview for government decision-makers.
